{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1557743-a-case-for-why-entropy-does-make-sense-for-necromancy","id":1557743,"title":"A case for why Entropy DOES make sense for Necromancy","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-01-18T15:57:37-08:00","id":1557743,"title":"A case for why Entropy DOES make sense for Necromancy","content":"So over the years, decades really, there has been a long standing discussion and consensus as to Entropy not making sense to be a focus of necromancers. Often by pointing out that it doesn't do anything to animate corpses or talk to/control ghosts.<br /> <br /> However that wasn't what old school real world occultism necromancy was for. Ok the talking to ghosts too admittedly. The thing is, like most things with &quot;mancy' in the name, it was meant as a tool of divination and using the dead to divine fate and prophecy is old. This fits quite well into Entropy, especially when the darker aspects of real world necromantic beliefs were often about inflicting curses or on the benevolent end are ancient beliefs of beseeching ancestors to ward bad luck and bestow blessings. <br /> <br /> One of the defining characteristics of MtA is that it tries to pull from real world occultism and the historic uses of necromancers fits very well with what Entropy, the Sphere of Fate, luck, decay is meant to carry.<br /> <br /> All that isn't to say that adding unnecessary mechanics to make it a more morbid Sphere is a good idea or that it is the perfect Sphere for necromancy when a lot of the other aspects of necromancy are covered by other Spheres. And by no means was real world necromancy the only belief that did these things. Just that it's not as nonsensical for the necromancy tradition to hold the seat of necromancy as so many people believe","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4178-stanlemon","name":"stanlemon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-18T17:14:57-08:00","id":1557745,"title":"","content":"I mean, the books themselves consistently moved away from Entropy being the &quot;necromancy&quot; Sphere pretty quickly because it makes minimal sense for a lot of things that get lumped into necromancy in modern sensibilities. I think it's very much the case that the Euthanatos having the seat of Entropy and being the most death associated Tradition is more responsible for the connection (in the official early books and fan impressions) rather than the kind of magic the Sphere allows. Entropy - necromancy or no - makes a lot of sense for the Euthanatos because of their strong view on fate/similar metaphysical concepts. The fact that they tend to be morbid and Entropy is generally more effective when destructive than constructive only makes the association stronger. The reason why the discussion tends to focus on &quot;necromancers&quot; instead is because the connection gets weaker when you move away from the Euthanatos and into other magical styles where things don't line up as neatly.<br /> <br /> I do think you make a good point about the idea of the dead being more of a Paradigm/Practice issue for the effect of directing divination (but even base divination is more Time than Entropy).<br /> <br /> But the game largely settled on a more &quot;crossover&quot; concept in the idea that in the WoD the Underworld is so imbued with the energies of Entropy that Entropy is necessary when deal with it on some level just like dealing with fae entities can require Mind, or Garou/Fera can require Spirit. So, it's more of a cosmological &quot;patch&quot; that doesn't require you to view the dead as something Entropy directly covers, but rather where the dead exist is so influenced by the metaphysical power of Oblivion that Entropy becomes an important Sphere for dealing with such matters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-20T08:25:02-08:00","id":1557780,"title":"","content":"As I recall, the word Necromancy is a sort of accidental pun, or really the mashing together of two words that had a lot of conceptual overlap and happened to sound a lot alike. One of the greek words for death (necros) is remarkably similar to a latin word for black so the two '-mancies' (death magic and black magic) were merged. Necromancy, then, wasn't limited to just the spirits of the dead but also to other spirits of the never-had-a-body variety, This would unintentionally include interaction with anything that falls under the purview of the Spirit sphere.<br /> <br /> One of the things I find fascinating about this one of the classic black and white magic was not a division between evil and good magic, because inherent to the definitions of the time all magic was wrong. Instead, it was a division between real and fake magic. 'White' magic in this context was the term for feats based around human trickery and deceit. So channeling the dead would be black magic, while pretending to channel the dead would be white magic.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-20T09:53:57-08:00","id":1557781,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1557743-a-case-for-why-entropy-does-make-sense-for-necromancy?p=1557780#post1557780\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Necromancy, then, wasn't limited to just the spirits of the dead but also to other spirits of the never-had-a-body variety,</div> </div> </div> </div>There's also the issue that Christian theology as it developed didn't really have room for ghosts. God sends you to your allotted reward and that's that. Summoning a ghost looks a lot like claiming to overrule the judgement of God which is impossible. Hence, if you summon a ghost successfully, it logically can't be a soul of the dead, it must be a demon tricking the summoner.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-20T11:16:02-08:00","id":1557783,"title":"","content":"The etymology of &quot;black&quot; and &quot;white&quot; magic is kinda fascinating but is so largely because it's also very messy and very speculative. While the conflation of nigramancy and black magic is pretty simple to trace... we don't really have recorded uses of them as interchangeable until the 1600s. That's a long time considering the amount of &quot;magic is bad&quot; from the Church that got written down and saved for posterity in the previous thousand years. It most certainly doesn't help that the most famous Biblical instance of necromancy was done by people that were translated as witches instead.<br /> <br /> And that's not even getting into the fact that Christian theology is hardly a monolith despite certain groups' efforts over the years. Dancing around how ghosts aren't a thing but saints can totally show up and help you out despite being dead is something many Christian theologians have had different answers on (and denial of saintly intercession wasn't really a mainstream thing until the Reformation).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-20T12:52:35-08:00","id":1557786,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1557743-a-case-for-why-entropy-does-make-sense-for-necromancy?p=1557781#post1557781\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> There's also the issue that Christian theology as it developed didn't really have room for ghosts. God sends you to your allotted reward and that's that. Summoning a ghost looks a lot like claiming to overrule the judgement of God which is impossible. Hence, if you summon a ghost successfully, it logically can't be a soul of the dead, it must be a demon tricking the summoner.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not exactly. There's no universal teaching in Christianity about the existence of ghosts (on earth) and opinions vary. There are some major Christian figures, like St Thomas Aquinas, who did think that ghostly visitations were a thing, including those who had been damned. So just because a ghost is outside heaven, hell, or purgatory does not mean it is not the real soul of the person in question. Though you are right, a demon pretending to be that person is also a possibility. Also if it is the soul of one of the damned, that's not exactly a trustworthy source either.<br /> <br /> I should point out that St Aquinas was not endorsing summoning the dead, he was merely talking about apparitions that happened without the aid of the living. He'd still consider summoning the dead wrong.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-29T05:11:56-07:00","id":1562297,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1557743-a-case-for-why-entropy-does-make-sense-for-necromancy?p=1557780#post1557780\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As I recall, the word Necromancy is a sort of accidental pun, or really the mashing together of two words that had a lot of conceptual overlap and happened to sound a lot alike. One of the greek words for death (necros) is remarkably similar to a latin word for black so the two '-mancies' (death magic and black magic) were merged. Necromancy, then, wasn't limited to just the spirits of the dead but also to other spirits of the never-had-a-body variety, This would unintentionally include interaction with anything that falls under the purview of the Spirit sphere.</div> </div> </div> </div>Late to the party, but I want to clarify this.<br /> <br /> -mancy comes from mant\u00e9ia, which meant divination, not magic. The use of the word to other forms of magic is a late development.<br /> <br /> Necromancy in its strictu sensu is divination through the dead, as Heavy Arms pointed, either by using a carcass as tool or contacting the spirits of the deceased.<br /> <br /> But then, this equally applies to every other kind of divination. We're not calling Entropy the Sphere of S\u00e9ances, or the Sphere of Seashells, Numerology, Astrology or whatever. Singling out Necromancy is nonsensical. As is saying that Time is the Sphere of Necromancy, Spirit is the Sphere of Necromancy, even though they are also linked to divination as performed by necromancers.<br /> <br /> Above all else: Euthanatoi aren't necromancers, and I think that's an even more important misconception in the game. They're healers and assassins, not the same thing.<br /> <br /> Entropy is their Sphere because of their whole &quot;care for the Wheel&quot; thing. Entropy is what they use to evaluate people in relation to the world, the community, their own destiny. And they use a lot of instruments for that.<br /> <br /> For killing they use guns. Killing through Magic is not a requirement or even particularly encouraged, and Entropy is present but pretty secondary in this regard.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}